# [[compatible|Compatible]], [[accordant]].
#: {{syn|en|conformable|compliant|congruent|Thesaurus:conformant}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Crusoe|page=43|passage=As I had once done thus in my breaking away from my Parents, ſo I could not be content now, but I muſt go and leave the happy View I had of being a rich and thriving Man in my new Plantation, only to purſue a raſh and immoderate Deſire of riſing faſter than the Nature of the Thing admitted; and thus I caſt my ſelf down again into the deepeſt Gulph of human Miſery that ever Man fell into, or perhaps could be '''conſiſtent''' with Life and a State Health of in the World.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|volume=I|chapter=XIX|page=251|passage=When I do myself the honour of speaking to you next on the subject, I shall hope to receive a more favourable answer than you have now given me; though I am far from accusing you of cruelty at present, because I know it to be the established custom of your sex to reject a man on the first application, and perhaps you have even now said as much to encourage my suit as would be '''consistent''' with the true delicacy of the female character.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Steven Sloman|title=The Battle between Intuition and Deliberation [review of ''{{w|Thinking, Fast and Slow}}'' (2011) by {{w|Daniel Kahneman}}]|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/the-battle-between-intuition-and-deliberation|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120108050233/http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/the-battle-between-intuition-and-deliberation|archivedate=8 January 2012|magazine=w:American Scientist|month=January|year=2012|volume=100|issue=1|page=74|passage=Libertarian paternalism is the view that, because the way options are presented to citizens affects what they choose, society should present options in a way that "nudges" our intuitive selves to make choices that are more '''consistent''' with what our more deliberative selves would have chosen if they were in control.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2026 March 18|author=Peter Plisner|title=Will devolution deliver real local control?|journal=RAIL|issue=1057|page=47|text=Rail Minister Lord Hendy was quizzed by the same MPs at an earlier hearing. He maintained that the Bill gave Mayors "much enhanced power" and preserved the existing processes by which rail services can be devolved. "The intention, more widely, is to work collaboratively with all devolved leaders to meet local needs," he said. "It has to be tempered by the reality, which is that the railway network is not generally '''consistent''' with mayoral boundaries. {{...}}".}}
# {{lb|en|logic}} Of a set of [[statement]]s: such that no [[contradiction]] [[logically]] [[follow]]s from them.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:William Spalding (writer)|William Spalding]]|chapter=Introduction|title=An Introduction to Logical Science: Being a Reprint of the Article “Logic” from the Eighth Edition of the Encyclopædia Britannica|location=Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:A&C Black|Adam and Charles Black]]|year=1857|section=chapter II (The Function and Axioms of Logical Science), paragraph 12(2)|pages=22–23|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=UW9GAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA22|oclc=|passage=When we ask whether ideas or terms are '''consistent''' or inconsistent with each other, the question really is, in what manner the relation presupposed between the ideas qualifies them for being combined as terms of a judgment.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Charles Petzold|chapter=Centuries of Progress|title=The Annotated Turing: A Guided Tour through {{w|Alan Turing}}’s Historic Paper on Computability and the Turing Machine|location=Indianapolis, Ind.|publisher=[[w:John Wiley & Sons|Wiley Publishing]]|year=2008|page=39|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=LFQ7DwAAQBAJ&pg=PA39|isbn=978-0-470-22905-7|passage=Part of establishing a foundation for geometry was demonstrating that the axioms were '''consistent'''&nbsp;– that they could never lead to contradictions.}}

