===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ped-}}
The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|fecchen|t=to get and bring back, fetch; to come for, get and take away; to steal; to carry away to kill; to search for; to obtain, procure}} {{nb...|facch, facche, facchen, fach, fache, fæchen, fatche, feccean, fecche, fecchen, fecchenn, fecchyn, fech, fechchen, feche, fechen, fechin, fechyn, fetche, fetchen, fetchyn, focche, focchen, fochche, foche, fotche, vacche, vacchen, vecche, vecchen, vechchen,|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=fecchen|pos=v|id=MED15419}}</ref> from {{inh|en|ang|feċċan}}, {{m|ang|fæċċan}}, {{m|ang|feccean|t=to fetch, bring; to draw; to gain, take; to seek}}, a variant of {{m|ang|fetian}}, {{m|ang|fatian|t=to bring near, fetch; to acquire, obtain; to bring on, induce; to fetch a wife, marry}}<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=69591|date=1895|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Lexico|alt=fetch{{sup|1}}|pos=v}}</ref> and possibly related to {{cog|ang|facian}}, {{m|ang|fācian|t=to acquire, obtain; to try to obtain; to get; to get to, reach}}, both from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*fatōną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*fatjaną|t=to hold, seize; to fetch}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ped-|t=to step, walk; to fall, stumble}}. 

The English word is cognate with {{cog|nl|vatten|t=to apprehend, catch; to grasp; to understand}}, {{cog|de|fassen|t=to catch, grasp; to capture, seize}}, {{cog|en|fet|t=(''obsolete'') to fetch}}, {{cog|fo|fata|t=to grasp, understand}}, {{cog|da|fatte|t=to grasp, understand}}, {{cog|sv|fatta|t=to grasp, understand}}, {{cog|is|feta|t=to go, step}}, {{cog|fy|fetsje|t=to grasp}}.

The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from the verb.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.{{sup|1}}''|noformat=yes|id=69588|date=1895|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Lexico|alt=fetch{{sup|1}}|pos=n}}</ref>

